# Which rcheevos the folder rcheevos/ beside this file is a copy of: the
# release, the commit it is in upstream's repository, and every file of that
# commit's tree with the id git gives its content there. Any checkout of
# upstream prints the same list with `git ls-tree -r <commit>`, so the copy
# can be held against it without a file being fetched.
#
# The copy is that tree with the patches below applied, each a diff of one
# upstream file kept in rcheevos-patches/, and nothing else: no file added and
# none changed in any other way. One file of the tree is not in the copy, on
# purpose and not by an oversight, and its line says `left-out` where the
# others say `file`. A comment of upstream's in it has two words that a check
# run over everything committed to this repository takes for something they
# are not, and the file could be spared: it belongs to the part of rcheevos
# that talks to the website, none of which is compiled here. That much is
# true of its neighbours in src/rapi as well, which are kept. Upstream's own
# test Makefile and Visual Studio project in the copy still name the file,
# and nothing here builds either. shared/tests/vendored_check.py holds the
# folder to all of this, and rcheevos-patches/PATCHES.md says what each patch
# is for and how this file is written again when rcheevos is upgraded.
upstream v12.5.0 1433173220a7eaede6a9ed7a18e94117be1821e0
patch 0001-wii-partition-table.patch
patch 0002-3do-directory.patch
patch 0003-playlist-in-playlist.patch
patch 0004-dol-short-reads.patch
patch 0005-short-headers.patch
patch 0006-gdi-bounds.patch
